    {
      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>In England, an Ofsted early years inspection is not a surprise if you have already run it on yourself. Early years provision in England is regulated by Ofsted, the counterpart to Scotland's Care Inspectorate, and not by CQC, which regulates health and adult social care. Ofsted expects settings on the Early Years Register to meet the Early Years Foundation Stage and to know their own strengths and weaknesses well enough to drive their own improvement. The real question is not whether to self-evaluate, effective self-evaluation is expected practice, but how to do it so the evaluation is honest, the evidence is captured at the point of observation, and the improvement plan is live rather than a document filed until the next inspection.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>In England, early years provision is regulated by Ofsted, not the Care Inspectorate, which covers Scotland, and not CQC. Wales is CIW and Estyn; Northern Ireland is the Education Authority and health trusts.</li><li>The statutory framework is the Early Years Foundation Stage, which sets both the safeguarding and welfare requirements and the learning and development requirements.</li><li>Ofsted no longer mandates a specific self-evaluation form, but expects settings to know their own strengths and weaknesses and to drive improvement.</li><li>An honest self-evaluation finds gaps early and builds evidence; it does not raise the grade. The manager acting on it does.</li><li>Quickler captures the self-evaluation walk, the ratio and safeguarding checks and the premises evidence. It is not a children's records, learning journal or register system.</li><li>Quickler is not affiliated with or endorsed by Ofsted, and whether your evidence satisfies an inspection is a judgement for you and your inspector.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The framework",
      "heading": "The Early Years Foundation Stage, briefly",
      "html": "<p>The Early Years Foundation Stage, the EYFS, is the statutory framework for early years provision in England. It has two halves that matter for self-evaluation. First, the safeguarding and welfare requirements: ratios, suitable people and safer recruitment, premises and safety, paediatric first aid, the key person approach, health, and food and drink. These are the non-negotiables. Second, the learning and development requirements: the areas of learning that make up the curriculum and how children build knowledge and make progress through them. Settings on the Early Years Register must meet the EYFS.</p><p>Ofsted inspects early years provision under its education inspection approach and reports a graded judgement. Ofsted has been reforming how it reports, so describe grading in general terms and work from the current published Ofsted early years inspection handbook rather than quoting exact grade labels, which are changing. Self-evaluation mirrors the inspection: the setting walks itself against the same themes, judges itself honestly, and records the evidence for each. The aim is not a flattering grade; it is to find the gaps an inspector would find, while there is time to close them. Treat this page as general information, not regulatory advice.</p>"
    },
