      "html": "<h3>What is nursery self-evaluation in England?</h3><p>It is the process by which an English nursery, pre-school or childminder judges its own quality against the Early Years Foundation Stage, honestly and continuously, and evidences each judgement. It covers quality of education, behaviour and attitudes, personal development, leadership and management, and the safeguarding and welfare requirements including ratios and safer recruitment. Early years provision in England is regulated by Ofsted, not the Care Inspectorate, which covers Scotland, and not CQC.</p><h3>Does Ofsted require a self-evaluation form?</h3><p>No. Ofsted no longer mandates a specific self-evaluation form, but it expects a setting to know its own strengths and weaknesses and to use self-evaluation to drive improvement. An inspector looks at how honestly a setting understands itself and whether it acts on what it finds. Always work from the current published EYFS and Ofsted early years inspection handbook, as they are updated and grading is being reformed, and treat this page as general information, not regulatory advice.</p><h3>How does an Ofsted early years inspection work?</h3><p>Ofsted inspects early years provision under the EYFS and its education inspection approach, reports a graded judgement, and looks at how honestly a setting self-evaluates and acts on what it finds. Grading is being reformed, so work from the current published handbook for exact labels. A setting that knows itself, evidences its judgements and can show live improvement work fares better than one filling in a form the week before. Quickler is not affiliated with or endorsed by Ofsted, and whether your evidence satisfies an inspection is a judgement for you and your inspector.</p><h3>Can I run nursery self-evaluation over WhatsApp?</h3><p>Yes. Quickler's workflow runs over the WhatsApp Business API. The manager or room lead receives each prompt in their existing WhatsApp chat, replies with text, a voice note 